<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rafael Antonio Gutiérrez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1845">1845</a>–<a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rafael Antonio Gutiérrez was a Salvadoran general and Liberal president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> from 1894 to 1898. He came to power through a revolt against Carlos Ezeta and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> in the Greater Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>. In November 1898 he was overthrown by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-regalado">Tomás Regalado</a>, who withdrew El Salvador from the union, which then collapsed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rafael-antonio-gutierrez.jpg" alt="Rafael Antonio Gutiérrez" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rafael_A._Gutierrez.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rafael Antonio Gutiérrez was born in Ilobasco, Cabañas, on 24 October 1845, the son of Marcela Gutiérrez and of a father who was a Spaniard. He married Carlota Mejía, with whom he had eight children; their daughter Marcela married a son of General Fernando Figueroa, later president himself. In May 1885 Gutiérrez backed General Francisco Menéndez in the revolution that overthrew President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-zaldivar">Rafael Zaldívar</a>, leading indigenous Salvadoran soldiers in Nahuizalco during the coup.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>On 29 April 1894 Gutiérrez and other officers rose against President Carlos Ezeta, who had let a financial crisis develop. With help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> the Revolution of the 44 deposed Ezeta on 9 June, and after a contest with General Manuel Rivas, who gave way, Gutiérrez became provisional president the next day. He was the only candidate in the 1895 election, winning 61,080 votes, and took office on 1 March 1895. He repealed several of Ezeta's crisis taxes, and Ezeta's attempt to return in 1895 failed.</p><p>His government dealt with disputes over the partition of indigenous communal land in Izalco, under a system set up in 1881 and 1882 that had taken land from the Izalco people; in 1897 he declared one partidor's partition invalid. He revived the University Council of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>, but expelled students who protested against him during the economic crisis of 1897, when he answered with railway building that proved unpopular. On 20 June 1895 he signed the Pact of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amapala">Amapala</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>, founding the Greater Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 13 November 1898 General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-regalado">Tomás Regalado</a>, a liberal who had helped him to power in 1894, overthrew him in a coup d'état. Gutiérrez and some of his supporters fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>. Indians in Izalco revolted the next day, and the union collapsed on 29 November. He stood for president in 1907 and received one vote. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> on 9 January 1921 and was buried in the Cemetery of Distinguished Citizens; President Jorge Meléndez declared three days of national mourning.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rafael Antonio Gutiérrez” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The liberal presidents Policarpo Bonilla of Honduras, José Santos Zelaya of Nicaragua and <b>Rafael Antonio Gutiérrez</b> of El Salvador signed the Pact of Amapala in June 1896, creating the Greater Republic of Central America.</blockquote><a href="/state/greater-republic-of-central-america">Greater Republic of Central America</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ilobasco, in what is now <a href="/countries/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.84,-88.85&amp;year=1894" aria-label="Ilobasco on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Rafael Antonio Gutiérrez"><image href="/maps/globes/g27000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5096 3828h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5096 3828h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.84,-88.85&amp;year=1894">What was here in 1894? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
